{
  "brief": "The code review assistant has demonstrably accelerated review cycles, cutting median pull‑request review time from 27 hours to 16 hours for the four pilot teams. An audit of 300 assistant‑generated comments shows that the majority (71%) are useful, though a non‑trivial minority (12%) are incorrect. These results suggest the tool adds value but also requires safeguards to mitigate erroneous feedback.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Median pull‑request review time dropped from 27 hours to 16 hours after the assistant was enabled for four teams.",
      "citations": ["S1"]
    },
    {
      "text": "An audit of 300 assistant comments rated 71% as useful and 12% as incorrect.",
      "citations": ["S2"]
    }
  ],
  "recommendations": [
    "Continue expanding the assistant to additional teams while tracking review cycle metrics.",
    "Implement a feedback loop for developers to flag incorrect comments, aiming to reduce the 12% error rate.",
    "Schedule regular quality audits (e.g., quarterly) to monitor usefulness and correctness trends.",
    "Provide targeted training or documentation to help reviewers interpret assistant suggestions effectively.",
    "Consider integrating automated validation checks to catch obvious errors before comments are posted."
  ],
  "draft_message": {
    "recipient": "Engineering Director",
    "subject": "Evaluation of Code Review Assistant Rollout",
    "body": "Hi [Director Name],\n\nI wanted to share a concise evaluation of the code review assistant pilot we launched with four teams.\n\n**Key Outcomes**\n- Review cycle time improved markedly: the median time to review a pull request fell from 27 hours to 16 hours (S1).\n- Quality audit of 300 assistant‑generated comments shows 71% were useful, while 12% were incorrect (S2).\n\n**Interpretation**\nThe tool is clearly accelerating the review process and delivering useful guidance in most cases. However, the 12% error rate indicates a need for safeguards to prevent misinformation from slipping into reviews.\n\n**Recommendations**\n1. Roll the assistant out to additional teams, maintaining the current monitoring framework.\n2. Deploy a simple “flag‑as‑incorrect” mechanism so developers can quickly report problematic comments; use this data to retrain or adjust the assistant.\n3. Conduct quarterly quality audits to track changes in usefulness and error rates.\n4. Offer brief training sessions or quick‑reference docs to help reviewers understand the assistant’s scope and limitations.\n5. Explore adding automated validation (e.g., linting, static analysis) that can pre‑filter obviously wrong suggestions before they reach reviewers.\n\nI believe these steps will let us capture the productivity gains while minimizing risk. Happy to discuss further or set up a meeting to plan the next phase.\n\nBest regards,\n[Your Name]\n[Your Title]"
  }
}

